# What compose itself reads for `docker compose up`: ports on the host, the database password, and
# where the settings file and the secret files are. Copy this file to `.env` next to compose.yaml and
# uncomment what you need; `.env` is ignored by git. Compose reads it on every `up`, and so does
# `make smoke`. Every line here is optional: without a `.env` the stack starts with the defaults
# below.
#
# The settings of the service go into another file, `.env.memini` (.env.memini.example), which
# compose passes into the container. Keep them apart: here MEMINI_HTTP_PORT is the port on the
# host, and nothing in this file reaches the service but the database password and the secret files.
# Never put a secret into either file; this one names the files that hold them.

# --- Ports ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The host port of the service (MCP at http://127.0.0.1:6364/mcp) and of the database.
# MEMINI_HTTP_PORT=6364
# MEMINI_DB_PORT=55432

# --- Database ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The password of the database role (default: memini), letters and digits only. Set it before the
# first start: the database keeps the password it was created with (compose.yaml).
# MEMINI_DB_PASSWORD=

# --- The settings of the service -----------------------------------------------------------------

# The file compose passes into the container; relative to compose.yaml. Optional: a file that does
# not exist is skipped, and the service runs with its defaults.
# MEMINI_ENV_FILE=.env.memini

# --- Secrets and other files ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Each names a file on the host, relative to compose.yaml or absolute; secrets/ is ignored by git
# for them. Compose mounts it read-only at /run/secrets/<name> in the container and points the
# service at it, so its content never shows in `docker inspect` (docs/configuration.md, "Docker
# compose"). Trailing line breaks are dropped.
#
# The service runs as uid 10001 and must be able to read the file. On Linux the file keeps its owner
# and mode in the container: give it to that user, e.g.
#   sudo chown 10001 secrets/credential_key && sudo chmod 400 secrets/credential_key
# Docker Desktop and OrbStack let the container's user read it as it is. After a change of a file's
# content: `docker compose up -d --force-recreate memini`.
#
# The key of the credential store, which encrypts the API keys and OAuth tokens of sources; keep a
# copy apart from backups (docs/operations/backup.md). 32 random bytes in base64:
#   (umask 077 && openssl rand -base64 32 > secrets/credential_key)
# MEMINI_CREDENTIAL_KEY_FILE=./secrets/credential_key
#
# Keys still read while `memini credentials rotate-key` runs, comma-separated (configuration.md).
# MEMINI_CREDENTIAL_OLD_KEYS_FILE=./secrets/credential_old_keys
#
# The token for https git remotes; MEMINI_GIT_HTTPS_TOKEN_HOSTS in .env.memini names the hosts it
# is sent to.
# MEMINI_GIT_HTTPS_TOKEN_FILE=./secrets/git_https_token
#
# The private key for ssh git remotes, and the known_hosts file their host keys are checked against.
# ssh refuses a key that others can read: keep its mode 600 or 400.
# MEMINI_GIT_SSH_KEY_PATH=./secrets/git_ssh_key
# MEMINI_GIT_SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS_PATH=./secrets/git_ssh_known_hosts
#
# The keys of the embedding API and of the LLM providers set in .env.memini.
# MEMINI_EMBEDDING_API_KEY_FILE=./secrets/embedding_api_key
# MEMINI_LLM_LOCAL_API_KEY_FILE=./secrets/llm_local_api_key
# MEMINI_LLM_CLOUD_API_KEY_FILE=./secrets/llm_cloud_api_key
#
# The bearer token that GET /metrics answers (no Memini token is accepted there); unset, the route
# does not exist. At least 32 random characters, e.g. from `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# MEMINI_METRICS_TOKEN_FILE=./secrets/metrics_token
